"""A partial model of multilingual grammatical fragments and boundaries.

Japanese explanatory endings and nominalizers are negative; commas depend
on preceding particles. Korean nominalizers are also negative, especially
after 는/은. Turkish conjunctions and some Japanese list/quantity markers
are positive. Other small rules cover dashes and attributive noun phrases.
Keep only 96 recent characters, resetting at document boundaries. This
leaves most content-word-specific variation unexplained.
