Control-flow and function headers produce large negative pulses at their
opening parentheses. Merged arguments weaken most pulses; backtick-rendered
code strengthens them. Loop declarations can echo on the next token.
Imports and exception-block openings have smaller negative responses.
`while True`, `for-in`, and tool-call JSON openings have positive responses.
